#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51d0cea4b75dffe5eef6ccf46e5156716eb1381d20b4193d811acfe83278094.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =                 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                                 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x >        0,$ while $\\mathbf F$  works against the particle for $x <           0.$ \n\n![](images/67/673798bb131da05a07b3f47894340cbbc143fa6a248823bcfbdf6b5a5238efb4.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/24/24884d31b2b9aa8d94b11bccfaab1fa31e62a34f4e5183676c79390923f1f243.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =      0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542" :question/problem "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165abac8-b78d-5d8b-9797-98764bec8a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459473} {:answer/id #uuid "9682a02a-0b80-592c-b204-2e00e64c9de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "165abac8-b78d-5d8b-9797-98764bec8a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8082197-4ec5-5193-873e-90d47f00746b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e01fbca2-a092-5b1e-95d7-018e7d1f5edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459475} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd8e7a9-c801-5eeb-bd2b-c483bdb34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd8e7a9-c801-5eeb-bd2b-c483bdb34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459476} :answer-field/id #uuid "422cd737-9b8c-53f2-a0cd-c12e1ad7286c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fbcc0ff-2235-56bb-aa08-904c6e3a229b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459477} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4988b-246a-5c24-99ad-02a2f31d8f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4988b-246a-5c24-99ad-02a2f31d8f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459478} :answer-field/id #uuid "07711a4e-3e3f-5c90-b74e-ec28eded9fbf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58d69dfd-31b1-5851-b7cd-9ea022ec672e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125380" :question/problem "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c75fe3-0f58-55e1-8f86-a88338d5cfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472520} {:answer/id #uuid "a60b9432-a58a-5243-9f31-007a718f9157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/va